0..77 Comment "// The circle as the book states it: one point, one loop at it, and the loops"
78..162 Comment "// at the point equivalent to the integers, `Path[.A = Circle](Base, Base) ≃ Int`."
163..236 Comment "// The loop is a free invertible cell at the point, so the paths from the"
237..313 Comment "// point to itself are the words over the loop and its inverse, with the two"
314..394 Comment "// cancellations as rules: the lowering mints them, and `Path[.A = Circle](Base,"
395..472 Comment "// Base)` is that presented type. The integers are presented the same way, so"
473..551 Comment "// the winding number folds the words into them, spinning folds them back, and"
552..629 Comment "// both round trips are proved by induction over each presented type, with no"
630..708 Comment "// interval and nothing owed. `loop-space.gandr` states the same mechanism one"
709..735 Comment "// stratum down, on terms."
736..738 Comment "//"
739..804 Comment "// The operations on paths are the book\'s own, each under `Path`:"
805..881 Comment "// `Path.step(p, q)`, `Path.back(p)` and `Path.ap(f, p)` stand in a body and"
882..957 Comment "// in a type, each reading its path\'s type off the words it acts on — the"
958..1035 Comment "// loop, a word, or another operation over one — and `Path.ap` its codomain"
1036..1081 Comment "// off the function\'s written result. Nothing"
1082..1153 Comment "// here defines them or annotates them. The alternative, each operation"
1154..1229 Comment "// written where a path type is stated, repeats what the words already say."
1230..1304 Comment "// Reversal: a cell between two distinct points, whose words spell neither"
1305..1369 Comment "// endpoint, would need its operations\' path type written again."
1371..1446 Comment "// The circle, the presented integers, the winding number and spinning with"
1447..1468 Comment "// their round trips."
